{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh saw that there was relief.</b> This expression is not used in connection with any plague except the plague of the frogs, for all the other plagues — once they were removed, they were removed completely; no trace of them remained after their removal. If so, there is nothing so surprising about Pharaoh's returning to the stubbornness of his heart, since what had passed was gone. But the frogs — even after the plague was removed from every place, it was not removed completely, for they did not simply go away; rather, they died, and the land stank. And he should have been humbled at least on account of the stench that remained from the frogs. Concerning this, Scripture says, as if providing an explanation: 'And he saw that there was relief, and he hardened his heart' — for a stench is harmful only in a confined space, but in a broad, spacious place the odor does not prevail so much. Therefore, when Pharaoh saw that there was relief — that the land of Egypt is broad and spacious, and the foul odor would not harm him — therefore 'he hardened his heart' as at the first."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:39.389154606Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}